# Student Planner server (IBM LinuxONE / any Linux)

`panzer_planner_server` is the shared backend for the Student Planner. It keeps everyone's calendar feeds in one place, refreshes them on a schedule, and lets a study group compare availability without anybody seeing each other's calendar links or class details.

It uses only the Python standard library (SQLite for storage), so it runs unchanged on x86, ARM and **s390x (IBM Z / LinuxONE)**.

 Student laptops (tkinter, "Server mode")            LinuxONE Linux guest
 ┌───────────────────────┐   HTTPS + token   ┌────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ panzer_planner v2     │ ───────────────▶  │ reverse proxy (TLS)                │
 │ panzer_planner_client│                   │   └─▶ panzer_planner_server        │
 └───────────────────────┘                   │        ├─ SQLite (planner.db)      │
                                             │        └─ sync worker ──▶ university│
                                             │             (every 30 min)   iCal URLs
                                             └────────────────────────────────────┘

## Files

| File | Where it runs | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `planner_core.py` | server | iCal parsing, recurrence, feed fetching (no GUI code) |
| `panzer_planner_server` | server | REST API, storage, scheduled sync, groups |
| `panzer_planner_client` | laptops | small API client helper |
| `panzer_planner v2` | laptops | the tkinter app |
| `Dockerfile` | server | optional container image |

Keep `planner_core.py` next to `panzer_planner_server` on the server.

## 1. Try it locally first

bash
python panzer_planner_server adduser alice        # prints alice's API token once
python panzer_planner_server adduser sam
PLANNER_TZ=Australia/Melbourne python panzer_planner_server serve      # http://127.0.0.1:8765


Then in the desktop app click **Server…**, enter `http://127.0.0.1:8765` and the token, and choose **Use server**. If you already have local calendars, the app offers to upload them.

## 2. Deploy on a LinuxONE guest

These steps assume a RHEL, SLES or Ubuntu guest for s390x with Python 3.9 or newer.

bash
sudo useradd --system --home /var/lib/student-planner --create-home planner
sudo mkdir -p /opt/student-planner
sudo cp planner_core.py panzer_planner_server /opt/student-planner/
sudo -u planner PLANNER_DB=/var/lib/student-planner/planner.db \
     python3 /opt/student-planner/panzer_planner_server adduser alice


**systemd service** (`/etc/systemd/system/student-planner.service`):

ini
[Unit]
Description=Student Planner server
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=planner
WorkingDirectory=/opt/student-planner
Environment=PLANNER_DB=/var/lib/student-planner/planner.db
Environment=PLANNER_TZ=Australia/Melbourne
Environment=PLANNER_TRUST_PROXY=1
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/student-planner.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/student-planner/panzer_planner_server serve --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8765
Restart=on-failure
NoNewPrivileges=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/student-planner
PrivateTmp=true

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target


bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now student-planner
journalctl -u student-planner -f


**TLS:** the app sends a token on every request, so serve it over HTTPS. The simplest option is a reverse proxy such as Caddy (automatic certificates):


planner.example.edu {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8765
}


(nginx or HAProxy work equally well. Alternatively pass `--certfile` and `--keyfile` to the server.) Only set `PLANNER_TRUST_PROXY=1` when the proxy is the only way to reach the server.

**Container option** (Docker or Podman on the guest):

```bash
docker build -t student-planner .
docker run --rm -v planner-data:/data student-planner adduser alice
docker run -d --name planner -p 127.0.0.1:8765:8765 -v planner-data:/data \
       -e PLANNER_TZ=Australia/Melbourne -e PLANNER_TRUST_PROXY=1 student-planner
```

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PLANNER_DB` | `./planner.db` | SQLite file |
| `PLANNER_TZ` | server's zone | Zone every time is shown in. **Set this** (e.g. `Australia/Melbourne`); servers are often on UTC |
| `PLANNER_SYNC_MINUTES` | `30` | Background refresh interval (`0` = off) |
| `PLANNER_SECRET_KEY` | unset | Fernet key; encrypts stored calendar URLs and headers (needs `pip install cryptography`) |
| `PLANNER_TRUST_PROXY` | `0` | Rate-limit by `X-Forwarded-For` when behind one proxy |
| `PLANNER_ALLOW_PRIVATE` | `0` | Allow fetching feeds from private/loopback addresses (testing only) |
| `PLANNER_HOST`, `PLANNER_PORT` | `127.0.0.1`, `8765` | Listen address |

Generate an encryption key once and keep it in `/etc/student-planner.env` (readable only by root and the service):

```bash
python3 -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print('PLANNER_SECRET_KEY=' + Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
```

Lose the key and the stored URLs cannot be decrypted, so back it up separately from the database.

## Users and groups

- **Users** are created by the administrator: `adduser NAME` prints a token once. `resettoken NAME` replaces it, and `listusers` shows who exists. Tokens are stored only as SHA-256 hashes.
- **Groups** are created inside the app (Study Group Planner → *New group*). The creator gets an **invite code** to share with classmates, who use *Join*.
- Each calendar has a **Shared** setting. Only shared calendars contribute to a group's availability.

## What other people can and cannot see

| Data | Other group members |
| --- | --- |
| Your calendar URLs and headers | Never |
| Class titles, locations, descriptions | Never |
| Busy time ranges (start and end only) from calendars marked Shared | Yes |
| Your username and that you belong to the group | Yes |

The administrator of the server can see everything in the database (URLs are encrypted only if you set `PLANNER_SECRET_KEY`, and the key sits on the same machine). Say so to your users.

## Security notes

- Users supply URLs that the **server** downloads, which could be abused to probe internal networks (SSRF). The server refuses non-public addresses at connection time, including after redirects, and ignores proxy environment variables. This is a best-effort guard. Run the service in a network segment that cannot reach sensitive internal hosts anyway.
- Feeds are limited to 8 MB, requests to 64 KB, and failed logins and invite-code guesses are rate-limited.
- The API has no password-reset or self-registration. All accounts come from the administrator.
- Backups: `sqlite3 /var/lib/student-planner/planner.db ".backup /backups/planner-$(date +%F).db"`.
- All users in one server share one time zone setting (`PLANNER_TZ`), and the app adopts it automatically in Server mode.

## API summary

All routes except `/api/health` need `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.

| Method and path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /api/health` | Status and the server's time zone |
| `GET /api/me` | Who the token belongs to |
| `GET /api/sources`, `POST /api/sources` | List / add your calendars |
| `PUT /api/sources/<id>`, `DELETE /api/sources/<id>` | Edit / remove one |
| `POST /api/sync` | Refresh all your calendars now |
| `GET /api/events` | Your cached events |
| `GET /api/groups`, `POST /api/groups` | List / create groups |
| `POST /api/groups/join` | Join with an invite code |
| `POST /api/groups/<id>/leave` | Leave a group |
| `GET /api/groups/<id>/busy?start=YYYY-MM-DD&days=7` | Members' busy ranges |

## Notes for IBM Z and LinuxONE

- The code is pure Python, so the s390x architecture (big-endian) makes no difference to it.
- OpenSSL on s390x normally uses the processor's built-in crypto acceleration (CPACF) for TLS and Fernet, with no code changes.
- `cryptography` may need to compile from source on s390x if no wheel exists for your Python version. This needs a Rust toolchain and takes a while. The server runs fine without it, you just lose at-rest encryption.
- A free trial environment for learning has been offered through the IBM LinuxONE Community Cloud. Check IBM's current terms.
- z/OS is not needed for any of this. It would only matter if you integrate with an official enrolment system that runs there.

## What has and hasn't been tested

Tested on x86-64 Linux with Python 3.12 and 3.13: the full API (authentication, isolation between users, privacy of group data, SSRF blocking, rate limiting, encryption at rest), and the desktop app against a live server (connect, upload, groups, free-time search, restart persistence).

**Not tested:** running on s390x hardware, building the Dockerfile, the TLS options, and running behind a reverse proxy.